trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Domain #6 (Social/occupational/exposure history) is now complete: postmenopausal status (8 years), no irritant exposures, non-smoker, minimal alcohol. This strongly supports consideration of genitourinary syndrome of menopause (GSM) — but persistent severe itching and progressive dyspareunia despite avoidance of irritants also raise concern for lichen sclerosus, which commonly affects postmenopausal women and can be underdiagnosed. Next, I must perform a targeted physical examination (domain #7). Key elements include inspection of the vulva for architectural changes (e.g., loss of labial architecture, phimosis of the clitoral hood, fissures, excoriations, white plaques, atrophy, hyperpigmentation), vaginal introitus assessment for stenosis or pallor, and gentle palpation for provoked vestibulodynia or induration. Since this is a clinical consultation, I will request the most critical, diagnostic elements of the exam.

[TEST] Please perform a targeted vulvar and vaginal examination, specifically assessing for:  
- Presence and distribution of white, parchment-like plaques or ecchymotic patches;  
- Loss of labial architecture, clitoral hood phimosis, or introital stenosis;  
- Fissures, erosions, or ulcerations;  
- Skin texture (e.g., thinning, fragility, telangiectasias);  
- Any areas of induration or raised plaques.  

Report findings in detail.

